# Docstrings

Every module, class and function gets a Google-style docstring, `__init__` and private helpers
included — those are where the non-obvious reasoning usually lives.

One-line imperative summary, blank line, then whichever of `Args:` / `Returns:` / `Raises:` /
`Yields:` / `Attributes:` apply.

```python
def get_child_logger(self, name: str) -> FilteringBoundLogger:
    """Create a logger that tags every record it emits with ``name``.

    Args:
        name: Component that will own the logger, e.g. ``"UsersService"``. Bound to
            the ``context`` key.

    Returns:
        A bound logger sharing this manager's processors and minimum level.
    """
```

Say why, not what — the signature already gives names and types. A docstring earns its place by
explaining constraints, side effects, units, and why a choice was made. Omit a section rather than
restating the signature in it (`"""Args: name: The name."""` is worse than nothing).

Pydantic models document their fields in a class-level `Attributes:` block, not per-field comments.
Reference code with double backticks; wrap at 100 columns.
